test:ledger-guards`, `gate-caller-audit` PASS. TEETH, 5 variants on scratch copies with the subject restored byte-identical each time: PRE-CURE verbatim reds 4 · `-unormal` reds 3 · the over-general `--ignored` reds its OWN reverse control ALONE · the LAWFUL short form `-uall` correctly stays GREEN · broken git args red 7.** ⚠️ **TWO METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, both caught by controls rather than by reading, and both are new silencers for traps this file already names. (1) A MANUFACTURED VARIANT MUST SATISFY ITS SUBJECT'S OWN MODULE-MAIN PREDICATE: `lane-usable.mjs:622` is a REGEX ON THE FILENAME (`/(^|\/)lane-usable\.mjs$/`), so a variant written as `.tmp-variant-xxx.mjs` never runs `main()` and exits **rc=0, 0 B stdout, no stderr** — indistinguishable from a subject that reported nothing. That is F-2215-1's silent-control trap with a new silencer: there a `/tmp` symlink, here a FILENAME. Name your variant what the subject expects, and copy its relative imports beside it. (2) MY FIRST REVERSE CONTROL ASSERTED THE WRONG BUCKET AND MISSED THE OVER-GENERAL CURE ENTIRELY: git prints ignored paths as `!! path`, and `classifyDirt` sorts only `??` into `untracked`, so `!!` falls through to the TRACKED bucket — **the bucket that DRIVES the DIRTY verdict**. The over-general `--ignored` cure was therefore WORSE than it looked (on the four `*-salvage` trees it would add 10,043 files of lawful build output, F-2486-1) and the obvious control was blind to it. Assert the bucket that has TEETH, not the one your finding is named after.** 🚫 **NO BOARD GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: untracked files in a live lane are a lawful routine state (F-1460-1). What IS guarded is our own CODE — a one-token flag that looks like a redundant default and whose removal is SILENT in every other channel, which is F-1667-1's lesson that an un-annotated cure can undo itself.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after F-2358-1 (*how WIDE is the selector?*), F-2389-1