cripts/withheld-evidence-audit-guard.test.mjs` — 11 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`, `gate-caller-audit` PASS; every arm reds under at least one manufactured variant (s2226's reachability duty), and the sweep FOUND A DECORATION: the lane-worktree branch — the very branch that decides `ON-DISK` vs `LOST` — was unreddened by anything until arm 11 was built for it.** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD NOTE PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE: my first sweep scored a variant as "reds all 10 arms" when the edit had produced INVALID JAVASCRIPT. A variant that cannot parse is a CONSTRUCTION REFUSAL, not evidence about the defect — it fails loud, which is correct, but reporting it as coverage would have credited every arm with teeth it had not earned. Assert your variant RAN before you read what it reddened.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is F-2357-1's orthogonal-axes lesson one rung down: those two instruments partition on WHERE a file lives and WHO cites it. This one partitions on WHO DECLINED TO COMMIT IT — a key neither can express, and the only one under which the loss is visible. When a component REFUSES to do something, its refusal is a signal with an owner; ask who reads it.** **Report them, do not merely trust them — this audit has now shipped a false zero TWICE, both times because its headline was narrower than the question printed above it. (F-1054-1, fixed s1054 `4051bf31`): it checked only whether a staging file's NAME was absent from main, so 36.54 MB whose name matched main but whose BYTES were in no object database read as `0 files`; it now classifies by blob hash. (F-1055-1, fixed s1055 `c8034384`): "in git" was `cat-file -e`, which asks only THIS disk — so the same 36.54 MB, sitting on a local-only salvage branch whose push had timed out, again read `AT RISK 0`; every in-git blob is now also checked against the remote-tracking refs. If you find a third such gap, fix the class, not the instance.** 🕳️ **AND THE THIRD GAP WAS FOUND — IN THE CURE FOR THE THIRD FALSE ZERO, WHERE THE INSTRUCTION ABOVE SAYS TO LOOK (F-2216-1, measured and cured s2216 — the THIRTEENTH consecutive fire to find a live instance of F-2204-1's class).** The audit's step 2b — F-1184-5's cure, which asks `git diff origin/main..main -- assets/` for assets committed to main and never pushed — sat behind a **bare `catch`** justified by a comment reading *"no origin, or no origin/main remote-tracking ref yet — nothing to compare against, and a missing remote is already the loudest possible signal."* ⚠️ **BOTH HALVES ARE MEASURABLY FALSE, and the first one is the catch's OWN NAMED CASE: it fires when the REMOTE IS PRESENT and only the `origin/main` REF is unavailable — and then `offsite` is still populated from the other remote-tracking refs (137 on this repo), so NOTHING is loud. The unpushed files simply never enter `scan` and appear in no bucket at all.** It fires for any other failure of that one call too, which the comment never contemplated — F-2212-1's class exactly: a handler correct for its named cause and blind to every other one. 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING on a scratch repo whose ground truth was ONE unpushed asset. Healthy git: `LOCAL-ONLY 1 files`, names it, `--strict` **rc=1**. Delete only `refs/remotes/origin/main`, remote and other refs intact: `LOCAL-ONLY 0 files`, `--strict` **rc=0**, `--json` `localOnlyFiles: 0` — ALL THREE MACHINE CHANNELS byte-identical to a genuinely safe board. Same for a transient failure of that one call.** The only dissent was an unlabelled line of **git's own stderr**, which no caller classifies and `--json` drops entirely — so per F-2211-1 it is not a signal, it is noise that happens to be true. ⚖️ **SEVERITY, STATED HONESTLY: on the live board the call SUCCEEDS, so every `LOCAL-ONLY 0` this streak has reported was TRUE — verified, not assumed. The defect is that the instrument is structurally incapable of telling you when it stops being true, and its consumer is not a gate but THIS LAW: the ART-SLOT duty makes every dry-board fire report that