ds 3 of 6, and each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it (collapsing 1 into 2 → arm 1 · declaring only on failure → arm 5 · refusing unconditionally → arms 1 and 6).** ⓘ **AND FOR THE FIRST TIME IN FIVE FIRES THE REVERSE CONTROLS DID NOT CORRECT MY PREDICTION — all four manufactured defects reddened exactly the arms I predicted. Recorded because the streak's running note has been the opposite; one clean prediction is not evidence the practice can be dropped, it is one data point against four.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the census question turned on a different axis: s2215 retired *"what does the TRY contain?"* in favour of *"what does the HANDLER do?"* — LOUD vs SILENT. That is right, and it found this site. But the polarity test is per-HANDLER while the harm is per-CONSUMER, and this handler has BOTH polarities at once: the same swallow makes `AT RISK` bigger (loud, self-announcing) and `LOCAL-ONLY` smaller (silent, a false zero). A handler is not sound because SOME caller notices; ask which number the LAW tells a fire to REPORT, and test that one.** 🔭 **AND THE `catch`-KEYED CENSUS IS ITSELF ONE INDIRECTION SHORT, MEASURED s2216: `spawnSync` NEVER THROWS on a failed child — it reports by RETURN VALUE — so s2212's *"46 non-test spawners with ZERO catch are structurally incapable"* is TRUE only for the throwing APIs. Measured: of 98 non-test scripts importing `child_process`, **63 use `execFileSync`/`execSync` (throw) and 28 use `spawnSync` (never throws), 18 of those with ZERO `catch` in the file** — a subject set every census in this streak has scored as incapable BY CONSTRUCTION. **Swept: all but one test `.status`, and that one is low-stakes (`gen-audio.mjs:194` reads `afinfo` stdout without testing either field, though it degrades to `null` rather than to a wrong number). The live decision instruments on this axis are SOUND and worth copying — `drain-block-check.mjs:320` (**was `:292` s2262–s2332 — re-based s2333, +28, when the F-2333-1 detached-HEAD refusal landed ~30 lines above it: the THIRD rot of this coordinate and the THIRD RUNNING caused by a fire editing the very script the law cites, predicted by this clause and cured in the same landing commit. Re-grepped, NOT arithmetic — the sibling pointer in this same file moved +76 in the same commit, so one remembered delta would have been wrong for the other**; was `:277` s2224–s2246 — re-based s2247, +15, when the F-2247-2 retired-reason-class exclusion landed above it; was `:212` s2216–s2223; re-based s2224, +65, when the F-2223-1 corpus-tree discriminator landed above it) is F-2212-1's own cure, and `red-inventory-lookup.mjs:64`/`:234` both test `.status` and degrade to `null` / an explicit `SNAPSHOT main commit UNKNOWN` line.** So this axis is clean TODAY, but it is not covered by the polarity test and the next fire should not assume it is. ⚠️ **AND THE POLARITY TEST HAS THE SAME BLIND SPOT EVERY CENSUS BEFORE IT HAD — MEASURED ON MY OWN NUMBERS, NOT PREDICTED. s2215 wrote that asking *what does the HANDLER do?* "needs NO reachability analysis, so it CANNOT be blind to an indirection BY CONSTRUCTION." That holds for the TRY side and FAILS for the HANDLER side: deciding whether a handler is LOUD means resolving what its calls DO, and a `process.exit` behind a one-line same-file helper is invisible to the token test.** 📊 **Measured s2216: of the `75` handlers the raw polarity test calls SILENT, **`4` ARE FALSE SILENTS** — `red-inventory-lookup.mjs:226`/`:256` call `fail()` (`process.exit(2)`), `blocker-panel-closed-guard.mjs:91`/`:113` call `refuse()`. TRUE SILENT after one pass of same-file wrapper resolution: `71`.** 🔑 **Two of those four are the exact coordinates s2215's handoff named as unexamined SILENT sites to sweep — so the aim I inherited pointed at two handlers that were already loud, and I found out ONLY because `law-pointer-guard` reddened on my citation of `:226` and made me read the line.** ⓘ **A guard caught a false accusation before it entered the law: that is the s1301 citations-run-LAST duty paying for itself, and it is why the coordinates above are `:64`/`:234` and not the ones I first wrote.** 💡 **So the ladder is NOT terminated, it has changed SIDES: file → lexical → same-file wrapper → imported helper, all on the TRY — and now wrapper resolution on the HANDLER. Resolve one level and re-count. Note too that my own cure ADDED a handler to this census (`102` → `103`), so a fire that edits a spawner moves the denominator it is measuring.** `worktrees/art/` is NOT a git worktree (no `.git`, absent from `git worktree list`), so its output is untracked-by-default and dies with the disk: F-1045-1 found **10 files / 16.5 MB** sitting in no commit, the oldest for **14 days**. That is a standing property of the slot, not one bad batch, and it is the RETENTION LAW's largest live hole. (2) Any ART refill must **cite the BACKLOG line proving the batch is unshipped** in the queued task or the handoff — art batches have been re-queued against already-shipped rosters (Mistake #8 shape).** 🧭 **AND BEFORE YOU SPEND AN AUTHORING BUDGET, ASK THE INSTRUMENT WHETHER ANYTHING IS AUTHORABLE AT ALL: `node scripts/authorable-candidates.mjs` (F-2153-1, measured s2153 — the tool has existed since F-1654-1 and THIS CLAUSE HAS NEVER NAMED IT).** It reads each `planned` leaf's structured `authoringBlock` (`class` / `finding` / `measuredBy` / `reason`), prints the refusal with who measured it and when, and closes with a census. **Measured s2153: `12 planned — 12 priced, 0 unpriced (owner-gated 8, attended-owed 3, needs-spec 1)` — i.e. ZERO fire-authorable**, so §2E's two-master dry-board allowance had nothing to spend itself on, and that is a FACT the tool states in one command rather than a judgement each fire must re-derive. ⚠️ **THE IRONY IS THE FINDING: the tool F-1654-1 built precisely so fires would stop re-deriving priced negatives is named in ZERO law surfaces.** Measured s2153 — `scripts/fire.md` **0** · `CLAUDE.md` **0** · `.claude/skills/author-task/SKILL.md` **0** · `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` **0**; it appears only in `STATUS.md` handoff archives and `tasks/BACKLOG.md` findings prose, **the two surfaces F-1654-1 itself proved a refilling fire does not read at refill time.** So F-1654-1's own diagnosis — *"a measured refusal had nowhere to live"* — recurred one level up: **the refusal-READER had nowhere to live.** 💰 **Cost, paid by the fire that wrote this clause and not inherited from anyone: s2153 re-derived `jumper-8way-wiring`'s refusal BY HAND through six probes** — goal-tree status census → `master-shipped-classifier` → reading the master → grepping `assets/LEDGER.md` for the `char.claim_jumper SHEET full-ROTATION` row → `drain-block-check` → an `authoringBlock` census — to arrive at precisely the verdict s1661 had already recorded ON THE LEAF **eleven days earlier**, one command away. ⓘ **AND `drain-block-check` WILL NOT SAVE YOU HERE — IT PRINTS `✅ CLEAR` FOR BOTH BANKED MASTERS** (`lane-c-jumper-8way-wiring.md` and `lane-e3-fairground-socket.md`, verified s2153), and it is RIGHT to: it answers *may I DRAIN this?*, and a `planned` leaf has nothing to drain. **A CLEAR from the drain guard is not a licence to QUEUE** — the two questions have two instruments, and §3.0's tool is deliberately blind to `authoringBlock`. Its sibling `node scripts/master-shipped-classifier.mjs` answers the other half — *is this banked master already SHIPPED?* (Mistake #8, the 824k Flail) — and is likewise named in no law surface (`fire.md` 0 · `CLAUDE.md` 0 · `author-task/SKILL.md` 0, measured s2153). ⚠️ **AND RUN IT FROM THE MAIN WORKTREE — FROM A LANE IT USED TO INFLATE THE QUEUE-ME SET 268× AND SAY NOTHING (F-2222-2, measured s2222, CURED s2223).** Three of its four inputs are TRACKED (`tasks/*.md`, the trace dirs under `tasks/`, `tasks/goals.json`), so a linked worktree hands it a board FROZEN at that lane’s commit. It now REFUSES there (`⛔ CANNOT VERIFY — DO NOT Q