r-fork`** is the block this sentence describes, while **`gate-side`** is a FIRE-RECORDED readiness hold ("this has not been gated yet") that **no owner will ever lift** — carrying one to the desk parks it forever. **Measured s1383 across all 9 blocked leaves: 5 owner-fork, 3 gate-side, 1 `disputed` (its own reason asserts both).** ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538: 6 blocked leaves — 5 owner-fork, 1 `disputed`, and `gate-side` is now ZERO (F-1538-2).** All three gate-side holds discharged the lawful way, by being gated and merged rather than by being re-labelled or dropped: the leaf s1383 named as the casualty of its own bug, `e1-authored-bundle-validation` — *"the cheapest real merge on the board"*, frozen because the label reached two of three leaves — is `status:"merged"` at `cbf0e143c043d82cc7eba8266a119f247e220718`, which is precisely the `cbf0e143` code-commit of the s1384 two-commit sequence recorded in the clause below. 💡 **The distinction earned its keep and then emptied its own bucket, which is the outcome to expect from a GOOD discriminator — so do NOT read `gate-side: 0` as evidence the class is obsolete.** It is a transient state by construction: any fire recording a readiness hold mints a new one, and the surviving 5 owner-fork + 1 disputed are exactly the residue that no green battery can lift. ⚠️ **Verify the fate before celebrating a shrinking census** — "gone from the blocked list" and "merged" are different facts, and only the second is good news; s1538 checked the mergeHash rather than the absence. The discriminator used to live ONLY in free prose, so it could be — and was — silently omitted: s1381's handoff records flipping "all three" of its leaves with an explicit *"GATE-SIDE HOLD, NOT AN OWNER GATE"* reason *"so the next fire cannot misread them as owner forks"*, and **the label reached TWO of the three**. The leaf it missed, `e1-authored-bundle-validation`, is the one **s1381 and s1382 each named as the #1 priority and "the cheapest real merge on the bo