d `~76%` before that**)** have no leaf, so a strict default would red the whole board rather than the one master you are asking about. 📉 **THE RATIO IS FALLING AND THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT'S REAL CONTENT, SO READ THE DIRECTION, NOT THE HEADLINE (F-1538-1).** Across s1276→s1538 the ABSOLUTE debt barely moved (526→538, **+12**) while the denominator grew by **179** — so **167 of 179 new masters (93.3%) were registered**, and the Goal Registration Law is demonstrably WORKING. The unregistered set is legacy debt, now decaying by dilution at roughly 4.5 points per 180 masters. 🔔 **THAT WATCH-THRESHOLD HAS NOW BEEN CROSSED, AND THE SENTENCE THE POLICY RESTED ON IS FALSE — RE-MEASURED s2100 (F-2100-1).** This clause used to read *"a 57.2% majority still lacks leaves, so a strict default still reds the board"*, and set itself a trigger: *"the number to watch is when this crosses below ~50% — at that point the advisory-default argument stops being about 'most of the board' and needs restating on its merits rather than on the count. Do not flip it early because the trend is pretty."* **Both halves have now come due: 49.5% is NOT a majority, so the justification is factually dead, and the trigger condition is met.** 📅 **It crossed at `176268426` (2026-08-20T15:12:39+07:00) — 541/1084 = 49.91%, with the immediately preceding commit at exactly 50.00% (541/1082)** — about 11 hours and 105 first-parent commits before s2100, during ordinary attended bookkeeping, which is why no fire noticed. ⚖️ **THE POLICY SURVIVES, BUT ON A RESTATED ARGUMENT — WHICH IS PRECISELY WHAT THE TRIGGER ASKED FOR, AND IS NOT A FLIP:** a strict default would still red **541 masters**, i.e. **half the board rather than the one master you are asking about**, so the advisory default now stands on the **ABSOLUTE COUNT** and no longer on "most of the board." 🚫 **DO NOT FLIP IT** — the restatement is the discharge of the trigger, not a licence to change the default. 📈 **AND THE NEWS UNDER THE NEWS IS BETTER THAN s1538 RECORDED: across s1538→s2100 the denominator grew +152 while the ABSOLUTE debt grew +3 — so 149 of 152 new masters (98.0%) were registered**, up from 93.3% over s1276→s1538. **It crossed by DILUTION, exactly the mechanism F-1538-1 predicted**: the debt is frozen legacy (538→541 in 152 masters), and the ratio falls because the Goal Registration Law keeps working. **The next number to watch is the ABSOLUTE 541** — if it starts climbing, registration has stopped working and that is a different finding entirely. ✅ **WATCHED AT LAST, s2182 (F-2182-1) — 82 fires after this clause set the trigger, and it is NOT TRIPPED: the absolute is STILL 541, at `541/1122 = 48.2%`.** Denominator +29 since s2100, absolute **+0**. 🔑 **AND THE SET IS FROZEN, NOT MERELY THE COUNT — WHICH IS THE STRONGER FACT AND THE ONE A COUNT CANNOT ESTABLISH: 0 masters newly unregistered, 0 retroactively registered, 0 removed.** An equal count is also what a deletion plus an unregistered addition looks like; the set diff is what discriminates, so **ask for the diff, not the tally, whenever a watch-number holds still.** 📈 **All 29 new masters registered = 100.0%**, up from 98.0% (s1538→s2100) and 93.3% (s1276→s1538) — the Registration Law is working *better* each window, and the debt is inert legacy decaying purely by dilution, exactly as F-1538-1 predicted and this clause restated. **Re-measure the ABSOLUTE, not the ratio; carry the trigger forward unchanged.** ⓘ **METHOD CONTROLLED AGAINST THREE HISTORICAL TREES BEFORE ANY DRIFT WAS ASSERTED — WIDENED TO FIVE s2182, ALL REPRODUCED TO THE DIGIT** (s1276 `527/763 = 69.1%` · s1538 `538/941 = 57.2%` · the crossing commit `176268426` `541/1084 = 49.9%` · s2100 `541/1093 = 49.5%` · s2097 `541/1093`) (`git ls-tree <rev>:tasks` × that rev's own `goals.json`, matching `taskFile` EXACTLY): at s1538's tree it reproduces **538/941 = 57.2% to the digit**, at s1276's **527/763 = 69.1%** against a recorded 526/762 (±1, one boundary master). ⚠️ **At s2097's tree it yields 541/1093 where that fire recorded 532/1093** — denominator identical to the digit, numerator +9 — so s2097 almost certainly resolved leaves through `drain-block-check`'s SUBSTRING match, which matches more leaves and therefore reports fewer unregistered. **Both readings put the ratio below 50%, so the threshold verdict does not depend on which resolution is preferred**; the +9 is named here so the next fire re-measuring this does not mistake a method difference for a real jump. ⛔ **THAT "almost certainly" IS NOW MEASURED FALSE — REFUTED s2182 (F-2182-1), AND THE SENTENCE IS KEPT ONLY SO THE REFUTATION HAS A SUBJECT.** Replicating `drain-block-check`'s resolver EXACTLY (`:234–257` — `normalize()` then bidirectional substring, then the `leaf.id` fallback) at s2097's OWN tree yields **478/1093, not 532** — delta **63**, not 9. Two intermediate candidates miss it too: `normalize()`-equality **539**, `normalize()`+id-equality **536**. **No candidate method reproduces 532; its true derivation is UNRECOVERED**, and naming a mechanism for it was a guess wearing a measurement's clothes. ⚖️ **THE PRACTICAL CAUTION SURVIVES AND IS UNDERSTATED SEVENFOLD, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS: the method-variance band at ONE FIXED TREE is 478–541 — 63 wide, not 9.** So *"do not mistake a method difference for a real jump"* is not merely right, it is **more** right than it claimed: a method swap can move this number by more than the entire legacy debt has moved in 900 fires. 💡 **REUSABLE: a hedge that names a MECHANISM invites the next fire to trust the mechanism and stop measuring; a hedge that names the measured BAND protects them.** When you cannot identify the cause of a divergent reading, publish the spread — not a plausible story. **Always re-derive with the EXACT-basename method above, which is the one controlled against four trees.** ⛔ **AND ROUTE IT THROUGH `node` — THE PRESCRIBED METHOD'S OWN INSTRUMENT IS REFUSED BY YOUR PERMISSION GATE (F-2427-1, measured s2427).** The method names `git ls-tree <rev>:tasks`, and **`git ls-tree main -- tasks/goals.json` run BARE returns `This command requires approval`** — as does bare `git rev-parse`, because the allowlist permits git PER SUBCOMMAND (`git log/status/diff/show/add/commit/merge/worktree/branch/checkout/push`) and neither is on it. 🔬 **CONTROL-ISOLATED, every arm run bare with no `cd` and no pipe, because a compound is a known confound (s2350): CONTROLS that RAN in the same shell — `git merge-base --is-ancestor` (§3.0's own probe), `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` (F-2389-1's binding triage read, itself NOT allowlisted by name), `sed -n <N>p`, `date "+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ"` (§1.3's binding stamp), `pgrep -f`, `which -a codex`.** So this is not a blanket state and **the allowlist cannot be read off settings**: two prescribed git reads run while two others refuse. ➡️ **THE FALLBACK IS THIS FILE'S STANDING ONE (§2.0d): `execFileSync('git', ['ls-tree','--name-only', rev + ':tasks'], {cwd: REPO})` under `Bash(node:*)` — the gate denies YOU, not the factory. s2427 discharged the watch that way.** 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED AS A SIXTH `COMMAND_FAMILIES` ENTRY to `law-bash-prescription-guard`, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: that guard's key must select PRESCRIPTIONS, and of the 5 `git ls-tree` spans in this file **4 ARE DESCRIPTIVE** (prose about what `master-shipped-classifier` does internally) and only this one is an imperative — so the pattern would mostly match MENTIONS, which is exactly the discriminator F-2365-1 established (*"the RUNNABLE COMMAND … is what separates a prescription from a mention"*). Adding it would also red nothing today, since this file already carries `execFileSync` and the guard is FILE-SCOPED. **The cure for the harm is the sentence you are reading, at the site, which is s2400's own conclusion for `ps`/`kill`.** ⓘ **`git rev-parse` and `git cherry` are measured refused too and are prescribed in ZERO law surfaces — 0 occurrences across all six — so they are not owed a fallback at all; recorded only so the next reader does not re-measure them.** 📉 **AND THE WATCH ITSELF IS DISCHARGED, 245 FIRES AFTER s2182 (F-2427-2): `539 / 1198 = 45.0%` — the ABSOLUTE **FELL BY 2** (541 → 539) while the denominator grew **+76**, so the trigger (*"if it starts climbing, registration has stopped working"*) is **NOT TRIPPED** and the sequence 69.1% → 57.2% → 49.9% → 48.2% → **45.0%** continues to fall by dilution exactly as F-1538-1 predicted. **Instrument validated against TWO recorded trees before its answer was believed (F-2215-1): the crossing commit `176268426` reproduces `541/1084 = 49.9%` and s2182's own tree `b4c797684` reproduces `541/1122` — both to the digit.** 🔑 **THE SET DIFF, which F-2182-1 rightly demands whenever the number moves: 76 new masters, 0 removed, **3 legacy debts RETIRED** (`ap-orders-adapter-wiring.md`, `art-batch-roster-e9.md`, `art-e9-buildings-a.md` — **precisely the three UNKNOWNs F-2206-1 and F-2208-1 resolved by file probe**, so the debt F-2208-1 warned would red `dry-board-probe --strict` at rc=2 is now genuinely gone, corroborated by this fire's own probe reading `UNKNOWN: 0`), and exactly ONE newly-unregistered master — `e3-fairground-socket.md`. ⚖️ **THAT ONE IS NOT A VIOLATION AND MUST NOT BE COUNTED AS ONE: it is a bannered SUPERSEDED duplicate whose taskFile key an attended session REMOVED ON PURPOSE the same day it was written (`note_s_att_20260823`, readable via `drain-block-check`), retained under the RETENTION LAW because we supersede rather than erase. Real registration is 76/76.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a measured LIMIT of this watch's own instrument: an exact-basename census scores a LAWFULLY leafless artifact as debt, so the ABSOLUTE can rise by one for a correct reason. A `+1` is a prompt to read the master, never a verdict that registration failed — ask `drain-block-check` before you alarm.** `--all` audits every blocked leaf on the board. ⏳ **AND RUN IT ONE STEP EARLIER IN TIME THAN THIS CLAUSE SAYS — BEFORE YOU WRITE A NEXT-FIRE INSTRUCTION THAT ASSERTS A TASK'S CLOSURE STATE, NOT MERELY BEFORE YOU DRAIN IT (F-1669-1, measured s1669).** This section's trigger is *the drain*, so it never fires for the fire that has nothing to drain — and that is exactly the fire that writes the handoff telling its successor what to drain. **s1665 asserted that `f1643-2` was a `gate-side` hold, citing as its evidence the DONE-MOVE FILENAME `stopped-s1655-gate-side-…`; s1667 and s1668 each copied the assertion forward.** The block had been lifted by **s1657 `9a32d94f2`**, whose diff deletes `status:"blocked"`, deletes `blockClass:"gate-side"`, deletes `blockedReason`, writes `status:"queued"` and **dispatches the master** — 1h52m after that filename froze. 🪦 **A done-move filename is minted once at done-move time and is NEVER renamed: it is a fossil of a claim the ledger can reverse, and here it did.** Measured across `tasks/done/`: **6 of 1270 filenames carry a closure-class token, 5 resolve to a leaf, and 2 of those 5 (40%) are contradicted by their live leaf** — this one (`queued`), and `…s1176-blocked-by-26-cell-master-divergence-…-eight-winds-wiring-hero.md`, whose leaf reads `merged` at `29bac3d9478e9f3a77385c75965b60447a90500c`. ⚠️ **The cost is not a stale paragraph — it is an armed Silent No-Op (Mistake #1):** the stopped run that filename names **left zero diff** by its own preserved record, so a successor obeying the instruction gates a run that changed nothing, and looks for a `blocked` leaf to flip that does not exist. 🚫 **No mechanism is proposed and the reason is the point: the obvious one — have this tool print the filename-vs-leaf disagreement — is cheap and purely mechanical and STILL would not have prevented this, because s1665 never ran the tool.** It had the filename in hand and read its words. **A tool only helps the fire that asks it** (the F-1582-1 gap: the misread happens while a fire reads with its eyes, a moment no battery observes). ➡️ **The cure is therefore the one command you already have, invoked at the moment of ASSERTION rather than the moment of action — and it generalises: any name the factory mints to describe a state (done-move prefixes, `save/` vs `archive/`, row markers) is a SNAPSHOT of a claim, while the ledger IS the claim. On disagreement the ledger wins and the name is what must be re-read.**\012**§3.0b CUSTODY — NEVER PLACE CONTENT YOU HAVE NOT *DECIDED* TO MERGE INTO MAIN'S WORKING TREE. GATE UNDECIDED CONTENT IN A DETACHED WORKTREE (F-1295-1, s1295 — MADE STANDING s1342).** ⚠️ **This cure was declared "adopted" by exactly one fire and then written into NO law surface for 47 fires** — re-measured s1342 across `scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**`, `AGENTS.md` and every `^- **s9` bullet in `STATUS.md` (**140 total = 18 genuine lettered law bullets** — `s9i`/`s9ab`/`s9al`/`s9ar`/… — **plus 119 numeric handoff archives and 3 bare `- **s9 ` VM-era env bullets (obsolete on native, per the blanket VM-era clause)**; the loose phrase "140 law bullets" would over-state the law corpus 7.8×, so it is written out here — ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538, 196 fires later: EXACTLY 140 / 18 / 119 / 3, every figure unchanged. DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS ONE AGAIN.** It is stable *by construction*, which is the part worth knowing: the `^- **s9` pattern matches only the closed historical range s900–s999, so it cannot grow no matter how many fires run — today's archives are minted as `- **s15NN`. A figure that looks like a growing census is in fact a frozen one, and this line now says so): the only "detached worktree" in the corpus is `CLAUDE.md:50` (Mistake #12), which is about **contention**, a different question F-1295-1 itself explicitly distinguishes. §3's clean-main rule asks whether *someone else's* dirt contaminates your gate; **this asks the inverse — whether YOUR gate leaks.** 🎯 **THE INCIDENT:** s1294 checked four lane paths into main's tree to evaluate a slice, wrote **VERDICT: HOLD — NOT MERGED**, reverted with `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>`, confirmed `git status` clean, and was **wrong** — a concurrent attended session's broad `git add` had already committed all four at **`b37c1fc6`**, under a commit message about a rehearsal that named none of them, **thirteen minutes before the handoff denying it.** 🔑 **WHY THE VERIFICATION COULD NOT WORK, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE HALF: every probe that fire had returns the SAME answer whether the revert worked or the content was committed out from under it.** `git status` cannot detect a sweep, because **a sweep's signature is the ABSENCE of dirt**; and `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` restores *from* a HEAD that has itself moved, so the "revert" **re-installed** the very content it was undoing. The only discriminating probe is a **blob-hash comparison against a PRE-sweep ref** — which is why F-1295-1 is recorded in hashes. ⚖️ **A HOLD verdict has NO ENFORCEMENT SURFACE:** it lives in a review file and a goal leaf, and **nothing in git defends it**. The same window ships an **owner-BLOCKED** slice just as readily — the version that costs a design fork, and the one §3.0 exists to prevent. **What must NOT be concluded is "the sweep turned out fine, so the sweep is fine":** that slice was exonerated by a control run *afterwards*, and had it gone the other way the defect would have shipped past a verdict that said no, with the fire that wrote the verdict still believing it held. Cost of the cure: one `git worktree add` plus a `node_modules` symlink. ⓘ **SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT ME: F-1295-1 measured the WORKING-TREE case only.** Gating by `git merge --no-ff` and planning to revert on a red (§3, "revert the merge") puts undecided content on **HEAD** rather than in the working tree — structurally the same bet against concurrency, but **UNMEASURED as of s1342**; treat it as a caution, not a finding, and do not cite it as proven. ✅ **NO LONGER UNMEASURED — THE HEAD-SIDE CASE WAS MEASURED s1589 AND THE CAUTION IS NOW A FINDING (F-1589-5).** s1589 left drain 2's merge staged in main's index while writing its review; **ninety seconds later** a concurrent Cowork agent committed **`9371c1c8d`** — parents `e15858b13` (the fire's own stamp commit) and `153d30ba6` (the lane tip) — sweeping the entire staged merge into a commit headlined *"the operator falls at twin-banks w16 (697 kills) — notebook gen 2"*, which names none of it. ⓘ **Nothing was lost and nothing was corrupt** (`main..lane/b` empty, artifacts byte-identical, the fire's other drain untouched) — **the casualty was attribution, and the EXPOSURE is far worse than the outcome: had that gate gone RED, the plan was `merge --abort`, and the sweep would have SHIPPED the rejected content anyway.** That is the same failure as F-1295-1 with the staging area swapped for the index, and it retires the "structurally the same bet" hedge above: it is not merely structurally the same, it has now happened. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: never leave a merge staged on main.** Gate undecided content in the detached worktree exactly as this section already mandates, then **merge and commit as ONE act** — `git merge --no-ff <branch> -m "…"`, never `--no-commit` followed by review-writing. The review file, ledger rows and goal leaf land in a FOLLOWING commit; that is already the house sequence (§3.0's two-commit rule exists because a commit cannot contain its own hash), so this costs nothing and closes the window. ⚠️ **Do not read "the sweep turned out fine" as evidence the window is safe** — that is the exact reasoning this section already forbids one paragraph above, and s1589 is the second fire to be saved by luck rather than by design.\012**§3.1 EVERY FIRE-SIDE PLAYWRIGHT COMMAND PASSES `--workers=1`. NOT AN OPTIMISATION — A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT OF THE INSTRUMENT (F-1270-1, measured s1270).** Until `d1a0846d` `playwright.config.ts` set no `workers` key, so a fire's default was **6 obtained workers**, and at 6 the fire shell's per-job CPU ceiling (F-1269-1: the ceiling is the fire's process context, measured 7.53× lane vs 3.47× fire at eight children) starves each chromium until timing-sensitive assertions go red. **Interleaved, same shell, same hour, s1270: `--workers=1` → 3/3 runs rc=0, 18/18 instances passed, 0 drift reds · default → 3/3 runs rc=1, 17 drift reds / 18.** ⭐ **And it costs nothing, which kills the only objection: w=1 mean wall 55.94 s vs default 60.74 s — the serial arm is 7.9% FASTER in the fire shell**, because the parallel arm is fighting the ceiling. ⚠️ **This is a factory-gate correctness issue, not a game bug: every fire-side gate ever run at default workers was a known-unreliable instrument, and its reds were the shell's, not the slice's.** `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md:33` has carried the flag since s114 — incidentally, with no stated reason, which is exactly why six fires never knew it was load-bearing; this clause states the reason so it cannot decay again. ✅ **THE LAW NOW HAS A MECHANISM, SO THE FLAG IS A BELT-AND-BRACES, NOT THE ONLY DEFENCE (F-1270-3, landed s1270 `d1a0846d`): `playwright.config.ts:50` carries `workers: isFireShell ? 1 : undefined`, keyed on `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` being PRESENT** (**was `:30`; re-based s1301 when the F-1296-3 `testIgnore` comment block was inserted above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts) — launchd sets it to `~/.claude-fires` and `scripts/fire-runner.sh:130` (**was `:129` — re-based s2500 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`129 → 130`, **+1**), the rot caused by the ATTENDED commit `304ff2396` (2026-09-04T12:45:12, the codex engine switch) landing MID-FIRE and inserting the WALL-on-primary echo directly above it. The cited line is INTACT and still supports the claim — re-read s2500: `:130` is the `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-alt"` alt-fire invocation, so `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` is still SET for fires and the `workers: isFireShell` key still holds. ⚠️ **Two sibling pointers rotted by the same +1 in the same commit (`CLAUDE.md` → `:136→:137`, `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` → `:129→:130`) — re-grep EACH, never carry one delta across three surfaces; they agreed here only by luck of a single-line insertion.** `:126`, `:121` same-day (+5,+3), `:102`, `:86`, `:81` before that; rotted attended 2026-08-12 by exactly 19 lines when the owner-ruled fire-engine switch landed above it, ALT line re-verified intact; before that rotted s1536 by exactly 16 lines when the F-1536-1 dry-board-guard cure replaced the hardcoded lane list at `:63`, and before that s1456 by 5 lines when the owner's `02bea28c` auth fix inserted the `.env.local` token block above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**) to `~/.claude-alt`, while lanes and attended sessions never set it, so **lanes keep full parallelism** (the lane shell runs 6 workers ~3.5× FASTER — F-1267-1 — and must not pay for a fire-only defect). Still pass `--workers=1` explicitly: it is free, it survives anyone editing the config, and it keeps the intent legible at the call site. 🚫 **What remains forbidden is pinning `workers` UNCONDITIONALLY** — a flat `workers: 1` taxes every lane run, and it passes a fire-only check while doing so, which is why the guard below asserts BOTH directions. ⚠️ **DO NOT "restore" this law by deleting the `workers: isFireShell` line** — an earlier revision of this very clause read *"`playwright.config.ts` sets no `workers` key"* and *"Do NOT fix this by setting `workers` in `playwright.config.ts`"*, both written **before** the mechanism landed in the same fire; a session obeying that sentence literally would silently re-open F-1270-1. 🔒 **Guarded since s1273 (F-1273-1): `scripts/fire-shell-serialisation.test.mjs`** in `test:node-guards` resolves the config in both env states and reds if the fire shell stops serialising **or** if lanes start being pinned. ⚖️ **A red seen at default workers is not evidence until it reproduces at `--workers=1`.**\012For a drain: `npx tsc --noEmit` + `npm run build` + the slice's own spec + affected adjacent suites + a boot probe (zero console/page errors, desktop + 390px). Full native runs — no splits needed. 🧮 **AND IF THE SLICE'S DIFF TOUCHES `src/sim/`, `src/systems/` OR `src/entities/`, THE BATTERY ALSO INCLUDES `npm run test:node-guards` (F-1460-1, second half of the cure; landed s1462 with the re-pin).** A slice-local spec is structurally incapable of seeing a **cross-cutting sim pin**, because the pin does not live in the slice's own suite — so a drain gated correctly and completely on the slice's specs can still move a number that another guard asserts, and nothing in the prescribed battery will say so. ⚠️ **That is not hypothetical, it is how F-1460-1 was born:** `4ab48743` (f1452-1 fort-solidity — gates the stuck-watchdog on `route.blocker` in `src/entities/Enemy.ts`, +139/-31 in `src/systems/BuildSystem.ts`) changed routing, therefore engagement, therefore **8 fewer kills over 20 waves**; it was deliberate and spec-green and reached main via drain `07213c73`, whose evidence reads *"4/4 own specs both projects"*. The Baron pin in `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` went stale that instant, `test:node-guards` went red **on every fire from 2026-08-04 to s1462**, and — the expensive part — **the red was wearing an excused class label (`cross-engine`), so for five fires nobody investigated it.** A red nobody investigates is worse than no test. 💡 **The general shape: a change is cross-cutting when it moves BEHAVIOUR the sim replays, not when it edits many files** — routing, blockers, targeting, spawn order and damage resolution all qualify while touching two files. Judge by the directory, not by the diff size; that is why this rule is keyed on paths a human cannot forget rather than on a judgement call. ⓘ **Cost, measured s1462 and stated honestly rather than waved at:** `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` is the battery's dominant term at **55.6 s** (its Baron test alone 8.5 s); the full battery is **284 tests / 281 pass / 3 skipped** and runs in one command. That is cheap against two days of a red board. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s1537 (F-1537-1) — every number in the previous sentence has drifted, and the DIRECTION is what matters: the battery is now `363 tests / 360 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped` in `181.3 s`, and `gr-sim` alone is `12 tests / 79.9 s` (44% of the whole), its two Baron tests `15.0 s` + `14.2 s` where the old text says one at 8.5 s.** ⚠️ **Do not read the drift as rot in the pointer sense — the battery GREW on purpose (+79 tests since s1462) and the s1462 figures were honest when written; what is now false is only the CHEAPNESS ARGUMENT'S ARITHMETIC, so it is restated rather than deleted.** ⓘ Practical consequence for the fire that has to schedule it: **3 minutes serial, not one**, and a fire that overlaps it with a second battery will contaminate both (s1536 hung ~19 min doing exactly that on shared `claimed-spec-harness-guard`/`fixture-teardown` fixtures). **Run it ALONE.** ⚠️ **AND THE REASON IS NOT THE HANG — A SELF-CONTAMINATED BATTERY MANUFACTURES FINDINGS, AND ITS RED IS NOT EVIDENCE IN EITHER DIRECTION (F-2462-1, measured s2462).** The sentence above justifies running alone with a HANG (s1536, ~19 min on shared fixtures), which is a COST and reads as a scheduling nicety a hurried fire may trade away. **The load-bearing reason is that a fire's own parallelism is INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A REAL TIMING REGRESSION AT THE ASSERTION.** 📊 **MEASURED s2462 on ONE tree, the only variable being what else was running: `test:power-budget` returned **p95 0.966 ms against a 0.500 ms cap — FAIL** while a playwright battery ran beside it, and **0.334 ms — PASS** (then 0.384 ms) alone. A **2.9× swing on a timing assertion**, against a slice that does not touch `src/systems/PowerGraph.ts` at all.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOTHING SHIPPED WRONG and this is not a false green — the contradiction was visible because the same tree was measured twice, and the cost