re reading a stale number under-budgets the duty by exactly the amount the factory has grown** — which is why this correction is now the third of its kind and should be expected to be re-taken again, not treated as final. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2166 (F-2166-2) — AND IT WAS RE-TAKEN, EXACTLY AS PREDICTED, ONE FIRE-DAY LATER: the battery is `503` tests / `498 pass / 0 fail / 5 skipped` in **`529.8 s`** — NINE minutes serial, not seven.** Fire shell, node **v26.4.0**, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` set, run ALONE on the merged tree of the `c1-assay-fairness` drain. **The sequence is now 284 → 363 → 424 → 472 → `503` tests and 55.6 → 181.3 → 280.9 → 404.7 → `529.8` s** — RESTATED, NOT DELETED, because the sequence IS the finding and the monotone direction is the whole content. ⓘ **This measurement also carries a second, load-bearing result: rc=0.** The runner that produced the slice reported `test:node-guards` **non-green** and blamed its own shell (node **23.11.1** against `.nvmrc` **26.4.0**, one 90 s `gr-sim` file timeout); re-run ALONE in the fire shell the SAME tree is **rc=0, 0 fail**, and `gr-sim` alone is **20 tests / 18 pass / 0 fail / 2 skipped / 265.8 s**. That is the F-2076-1 mechanism confirmed a third time from the other direction — **v23 bounds at FILE granularity and never consults per-test budgets** — so a lane-side red on this battery is a question about WHICH INTERPRETER RAN IT before it is a question about the slice. **The drain's own re-run is the free control on the runner's headline; take it before you inherit a red.** ⚖️ **AND DO NOT INHERIT "the battery reds in the fire shell" FROM F-2076-1 — THAT RED IS LOAD-ATTRIBUTABLE, MEASURED, NOT STRUCTURAL.** All three tests it named as budget-endangered PASSED here with 2.0–2.4× more headroom than it recorded one day earlier: `runtime rush` (90 s budget) **90,003 ms CANCELLED at s2076 → 46,297 ms PASS (51.4%)**, `overtime`-on-both-engines (240 s) **219,418 ms (91.4%) → 91,282 ms (38.0%)**, `Twin Banks` (45 s) **34,896 ms (77.5%) → 16,275 ms (36.2%)**. F-2076-1's MECHANISM stands unrefuted and is the durable half (v26 consults per-test budgets, v23 bounds at FILE granularity and never consults them); what is now measured is that the MARGIN moves with machine load, so a red here is a question about the arrangement before it is a question about the sim — **`sample` the pid and check the load before theorising, and never re-pin to make it go away.** ⚖️ **A failure here is a FINDING, never a re-pin reflex** — see the standing prohibition below and the comment at the pin site: re-pin only with a **named cause**, never to make a red go away (F-1441-3). **Merge-throughput tools (2026-07-06): (a) DISJOINT-PAIR BATCH — two lane drains may share ONE gate battery when their `git diff --name-only` sets vs their bases are disjoint (additive-only Balance.ts / vite-env.d.ts hunks excepted): merge both onto clean main, run one combined battery (both slices' specs + the union of adjacent suites), commit each path-scoped. On ANY failure revert both and fall back to strict serial. Never batch more than two; never batch when one touches files the other's spec exercises. (b) ⛔ **WITHDRAWN s1270 — this clause prescribed the exact thing §3.1 forbids.** It read: "the desktop and mobile playwright projects may run concurrently on two scratch ports when wall-time matters." Both of its premises are now measured false in the fire shell: concurrency is what MANUFACTURES the reds (17 drift reds / 18 at default workers vs 0 / 18 at `--workers=1`), and wall-time does not favour it anyway (**60.74 s concurrent vs 55.94 s serial**). Run the projects SERIALLY under `--workers=1`. The scratch-port family (5199/5231/5234) stays — that is Mistake #12 attribution hygiene and is unrelated. The gate CONTENT is never thinned — these optimize repetition, not rigor.** For lane drains: diff the worktree against ITS base commit (find it via the lane branch), classify LANE-TOUCHED vs MAIN-MOVED-ONLY per file, never blind-copy —