D IS ON THE DESK AS F-2343-2: a launchd job for the runner (or a scheduled `health-watch.sh watch`, whose remediation arm already exists) would make this self-healing across reboots. That is a permanent job on Robin's machine, so a fire does not install one — it restarts the runner and asks.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it sharpens the streak's own question: twenty fires have asked *how can this subject set come back EMPTY?* — a throw, a guard, a `catch`, a stale tree, a cwd. This is the axis under all of them: AN EMPTY QUEUE IS THE SHAPE OF A HEALTHY BOARD AND ALSO THE SHAPE OF A BOARD NOTHING CAN CONSUME, and no probe that reads only the WORK can tell them apart. When every instrument agrees the board is at rest, ask once whether anything is still ALIVE to move it — the question is about the CONSUMER, not the corpus, and it costs one command.**\012§2.0d ⛔ **AND THAT ONE COMMAND IS REFUSED BY YOUR OWN PERMISSION GATE — EVERY `bash scripts/*.sh` THIS LAW PRESCRIBES IS UNRUNNABLE IN THE FORM IT IS WRITTEN, INCLUDING §2.0c's BINDING TRIAGE READ AND §2.0b's EMERGENCY RESTART (F-2350-1, measured s2350).** §2.0c landed ONE FIRE EARLIER and makes `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` a duty on EVERY fire. **s2350 typed it as prescribed and got `This command requires approval`.** 🔬 **CONTROL-ISOLATED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED, because the first two refusals were compound commands and `cd … && …` is a confound: the BARE form `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status`, no `cd`, no pipe, is refused on its own, while compound commands built from allowlisted binaries (`cd … && git …`, `cd … && node …`) passed dozens of times in the same fire. The refused token is `bash` itself.** 📊 **MEASURED, not inferred: `89` allow entries across the two settings files — `74` in `.claude/settings.json` (TRACKED) and `15` in `.claude/settings.local.json` (untracked) — and **ZERO** permit `bash`. `Bash(node:*)` IS allowed.** ⚙️ **IT IS STRUCTURAL FOR EVERY FIRE, NOT AN ARTIFACT OF ONE SESSION: `fire-runner.sh` invokes `"$CLAUDE_BIN" -p "$(cat scripts/fire.md)" --model "$FIRE_MODEL"` with NO `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, so every fire that has ever run has been under this gate.** 📋 **BLAST RADIUS, censused over the six `LAW_SURFACES` imported from `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs` (not transcribed): `8` prescribed `bash scripts/*.sh` invocations across `3` surfaces — `scripts/fire.md` 6, `CLAUDE.md` 1, `.claude/skills/playtest-intake/SKILL.md` 1 — naming `5` distinct scripts: `health-watch.sh`, `start-lane-runner.sh`, `lane-runner-v3.sh`, `deploy.sh`, `dashboard-gen.sh`.** ⛔ **ONE OF THOSE FIVE IS A CARVE-OUT AND THE GENERIC FALLBACK MUST NOT BE APPLIED TO IT — `deploy.sh` IS `PUBLISH-GATED: DO NOT WRAP` (F-2371-6, filed by s2371 against ITSELF for wrapping it; DISCHARGED s2372 by the next fire, which hit the same case and took the carve-out instead).** Every other refused script in this census is a READ or a LOCAL REPAIR, where a permission stop costs the factory a duty and the node route restores it — that is the whole harm §2.0d exists to prevent. **`deploy.sh` PUBLISHES TO THE PUBLIC WEB.** There a permission stop is not an obstacle to route around: **it IS the owner's live control over what reaches the family's browser**, and the standing fire memory has said since s1332 that wrapping deploy is the one case where the node trick must not be used. ➡️ **THEREFORE: attempt `bash scripts/deploy.sh` exactly as DEPLOY LAW prescribes; if the gate refuses, DO NOT re-run it through `node` — record the refusal in your handoff and let the owner publish.** ⓘ **s2372 executed precisely this: it merged gameplay-affecting code (`a73790d9a`), attempted the prescribed form, was refused, and did NOT wrap it — the first fire to take the carve-out prospectively rather than discover it afterwards.** ⚖️ **Note what this does NOT weaken: the content was still fully gated and merged, and nothing about the drain waits on the owner. Only PUBLICATION does, which is the point.** 🔒 **MECHANISED SO IT CANNOT BE ARGUED AWAY, which was F-2371-6's own stated condition (*"it must be expressible to `law-bash-prescription-guard` or it will be argued away"*): that guard now carries a `PUBLISH_GATED` set and REDS if a surface prescribing `deploy.sh` loses this carve-out token, so deleting the sentence breaks the battery rather than quietly restoring the defect.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: this is NOT a false green and NOTHING silently returned a wrong answer — the refusal is LOUD, and s2350 discharged both duties anyway via the fallback below. `deploy.sh`'s case was already known (the DEPLOY-law memory), so the class is not new.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A CLAUSE IS THE DIRECTION AT THE TWO MOMENTS THAT MATTER: a refusal reads as "I cannot do this", and §2.0c's read fails toward SKIPPING the liveness check — the exact silent blind spot §2.0c exists to close and prices at 37h50m / 35 fires; §2.0b's restart fails toward the OUTAGE PERSISTING, with the fire holding the only cure and unable to type it.** ➡️ **THE FALLBACK, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING YOU NEED FROM THIS CLAUSE — route through `node`, which IS allowlisted (the gate denies YOU, not the factory):** `node -e "const{execFileSync}=require('child_process');console.log(execFileSync('bash',['scripts/health-watch.sh','status'],{encoding:'utf8'}))"` — and the same shape for `start-lane-runner.sh --check`, for `npm run test:ledger-guards` (whose own battery chains SIX `bash scripts/*.test.sh` legs), and for every other refused leg. **Both were exercised s2350: `health-watch` returned `runner : ALIVE`, and `start-lane-runner.sh --check` returned `rc=0, 1013 B` reporting `codex 0.149.1` and `environment is READY; started nothing`.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS THIS FACTORY'S MOST-REPEATED FINDING FOR THE THIRD TIME IN THE SAME FILE: THE CURE ALREADY EXISTED, IN THIS LAW, ONE SECTION UP, AND THE NEW CLAUSES NEVER ADOPTED IT.** §2.0 has said since s1640 — *"invoke it through **node** (`execFileSync`), because the bash gate refuses a direct binary path (measured s1640; the gate denies YOU, not the factory)"* — and §2.0b (s1653) and §2.0c (s2343) were both written afterwards, both prescribe `bash …`, and **neither carries the word `node`.** That is F-2153-1 (`authorable-candidates` named in zero law surfaces) and F-2204-1 (the `FIRE END` lock signal parked in untracked memory) a third time: **a known cure is only as good as the surface a fire reads AT THE MOMENT IT APPLIES.** When you write a clause prescribing a command, type the command and run it before you land the clause. 🔺 **THE PERSISTENT FIX IS AN OWNER CALL AND IS ON THE DESK AS F-2350-3: adding `Bash(bash scripts/*)` to `.claude/settings.json` would make the prescribed forms simply work. That file is TRACKED, so a fire editing it would be SELF-GRANTING its own permissions — a thing a fire must not do (§7.3). The node fallback costs nothing and needs no ruling, so the factory is not blocked either way.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/law-bash-prescription-guard.test.mjs`: for every `bash scripts/*.sh` a law surface prescribes, either `bash` is allowlisted (and the prescription is runnable as written) or that surface must carry the node fallback. Mechanical, self-extending, and it retires itself the day the allowlist changes.** ⏫ **WIDENED s2359 (F-2359-1) — AND THE WIDENING IS THE LESSON: THAT GUARD SELECTED ON ONE COMMAND FAMILY WHILE THE CLAUSE ABOVE NAMES TWO.** This section has told you since s2350 to route `npm run test:ledger-guards` through node — and the guard censused only `bash scripts/*.sh`, so it passed **8/8 green while blind to the npm half of its own finding**. 🔬 **MEASURED s2359 with the controls isolated, because `cd … && …` is a known confound: `npm run test:ledger-guards` and `npm run test:node-guards`, BARE and unpiped, both return `This command requires approval`; CONTROL `npm -v` runs and prints `11.17.0`; and the node fallback returns **rc=0, 871 node assertions + 83 bash legs**. So it is not `npm` that is refused — `npm run build`, `npm test` and `npm -v` are all allowlisted — it is these two forms, which are the batteries F-1300-4 and F-1460-1 make mandatory.** ⚖️ **NOT a false green and the realised cost is ZERO: this surface DOES state the npm fallback, so its reader was served. What was missing was the mechanism — the protection was a convention nothing enforced, which is exactly how the `fire-runner.sh` pointer rotted at s1456.** ➡️ **The guard now censuses BOTH families (12 arms) and retires each one independently as the allowlist grows. 💡 REUSABLE: when a finding names N members of a class and its guard enumerates one, the guard is not a partial cure — IT CERTIFIES THE UNCOVERED MEMBERS AS CLEAN.**\012Drains within one fire remain STRICTLY SERIAL: each one fully gated + committed before the next begins (never batch-gate). After each completed drain, refresh your line-1 ACTIVE stamp (keeps the 45-min staleness check honest) and re-triage from the top — a new failed run or fresher priority may outrank the queue you planned. Non-drain actions (bookkeeping, correctives, re-queues) don't count against the budget.\012§2H **HEAT ARENAS:** prepare every detached arena with `npm ci`, never `npm install`, and prove the arena clean with `git status --short` before minting its engine probe. `npm ci` installs the committed dependency tree without rewriting the lockfile; the narrowed engine identity excludes package and lock files, so an accidental lockfile rewrite no longer rotates a reel's identity either.\012A. **Uncommitted bookkeeping** (host-side STATUS/task edits from attended sessions): commit them first — this may be your whole first act some fires. **ATTENDED-COEXISTENCE LAW (s61 correction, 2026-07-07 ~12:55, after a 74-min drain stall): "attended session live" is NOT a no-drain condition — that over-politeness starved the board while the owner watched. The precise rule: drains are blocked only by WHO OWNS THE DIRT. Check `git status` tracked-dirt ownership: runner output (src/e2e files from a done-move) = DRAINABLE regardless of attended activity; attended dirt (docs/tasks/specs/logs) = disjoint, drain anyway with path-scoped commits; STOP only if attended holds the STATUS line-1 lock or uncommitted src/ edits exist that belong to NO done-move. When in doubt: drain the runner's output, leave the attended files alone.** **AMENDMENT (s61, 2026-07-08 morning, after the 29-stand-down night jam): three clarifications with the force of law — (1) MAIN-SLOT runner output is ALWAYS uncommitted working-tree dirt + a done-move file; the runner auto-commits LANES ONLY. Uncommitted src WITH a matching done-move in tasks/done/ = runner output = DRAINABLE. The heuristic "uncommitted src = attended hand-work" is FALSE for the main slot and caused a full night of blocked drains. (2) done/ task files are ALWAYS untracked — untracked done-moves prove nothing about authorship. (3) a RESIDENT attended pid with zero fs-writes >30 min = IDLE, not live-driving; idle attended NEVER blocks drains. Match dirt to done-moves by the task's TOUCH-ONLY list; drain what matches.** **POST-WALL RECOVERY LAW (s61, 2026-07-08 ~15:30, after SIX vanished casualties — 055/057/ac-02/e2-science-tree/ss-02/audio-002 all lost silently to wall gaps + lane resets): the FIRST fire after any codex wall/auth gap runs a mandatory recovery sweep — (1) every rc1/CRASHED in tasks/failed/ from the gap window: verify the master's content against main by FILE-PROBE (never memory/messages); absent → re-queue the master; (2) every done-move of the prior 6 hours: same verification; (3) write the sweep's findings into the handoff. Walls eat silently; the sweep is not optional.** **BACKUP LAW (owner ruling 2026-07-07): if `git remote get-url origin` succeeds, `git push origin main` after every handoff commit (plus current lane branches weekly). Never force-push — force is deny-listed. If the push fails (auth/network), note it in the handoff and continue; backup failures never block drains.** **DEPLOY LAW (owner ruling 2026-07-07): after the backup push, IF this fire MERGED gameplay-affecting code, run `bash scripts/deploy.sh` — it self-skips when wrangler/auth/project are missing and never blocks; the family plays the latest gated build at the Pages URL. Bookkeeping-only fires skip deploy.** **DEPLOY MIRROR LAW:** /opt/goldrush is a positive runtime allowlist; a new runtime dependency means extending that allowlist and passing deploy-mirror-allowlist.test.mjs, never adding an exclude.\012B. **Runner output to drain**: done-moves in tasks/done/ newer than the last handoff, or dirty main from a finished main-slot task → GATE IT (see §3). One drain per fire. Priority: main-slot output > lane done-moves (serial, oldest first) > art raws processing. **EXCEPTION (owner-priority, 2026-07-06): a lane drain flagged OWNER-PRIORITY in HANDOVER §4 outranks main-slot output — starving owner-named work is worse than a one-fire main delay. Currently flagged: lane-a demo-profiles (Demo Day; Robin asked by name).**\012C. **Failed runs**: tasks/failed/ entries whose run log tail shows "turn interrupted" + large token count = Codex credit wall → re-queue from tasks/<name>.md master, at most once per fire; two consecutive interrupts on the same task = STOP and flag Robin in the handoff. Any other rc≠0: READ the log before deciding (real failures get findings written, not blind retries).\012D. **Standing corrections owed** (check STATUS "Next fires" and "owes" lists): test correctives, LEDGER upkeep, contract wiring for processed art, spec refreshes. **Plus the ASSAYER duty (standing, 2026-07-06): if `assets/crafting-queue/pending/` is non-empty, verdict ONE order this fire — generate the item WITHIN the profile's epoch contract (currently contract.v1: vocabulary, stat caps), validate contractVerdict + simVerdict honestly (canon §9.2/9.4 applies — no firearms, naming rules), write `approved/<id>.json` or `rejected/<id>.json` matching the 033 fixture shapes exactly (they are the reference), one-paragraph note in the handoff. Generator proposes, contract disposes — reject rather than stretch vocabulary. Item APPLICATION stays unwired until its slice, so verdicts are display-safe. First live case: Robin's multi-target Spark Rig order (07:16Z).** 🔎 **AND BEFORE YOU TREAT ANY `stopped`/`superseded`/`blocked` LEAF AS AN OPEN THREAD, ASK THE INSTRUMENT INSTEAD OF READING THE JSON: `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs <taskfile>` PRINTS THE CLOSURE REASON UNDER EVERY SPELLING (F-1582-1, s1582).** It resolves across all six accepted keys plus the `note_s*` family and prints the whole note, the refusal arm, the BACKLOG context line and what to do about it; `--all` lists every blocked leaf AND every terminal-closed one by name. ⚠️ **NEVER conclude "no record" from the absence of one field name.** That is F-1261-4 — refuted in s1274, guarded ever since by `scripts/goal-closure-reason.test.mjs` — and it recurred anyway: s1580 flagged `eight-winds-wiring-e2-enemies` as an orphan for having *"no `drainNotes` at all"*, s1581 carried it, s1582 answered it in one command from a `stopNote` + `reason` that had been sitting there since s1324. ⓘ **Field-name lookup is the wrong instrument for every reader, every time — measured s1582: 27 distinct closure-field spellings across 723 leaves** (`drainNotes` 113 · `note` 71 · `reason` 40 · `stopNote` 4 · `drainNote` 3 · `notes` 3 · `closedReason` 1 · ~35 one-off `note_sNNNN`). 💡 **The guard for this was green the whole time and prevented nothing, because the misreading happens while a fire reads JSON with its eyes during triage — a moment no battery observes. That is why this is a pointer and NOT a new mechanism: a second resolver would duplicate `drain-block-check`'s and re-commit the very sprawl the existing guard's header warns against.**\012E. **PIPELINE REFILL (owner directive 2026-07-06: "4–5 lanes working constantly")**: after drains, check every lane queue; for each EMPTY queue, `cp` the top unblocked item from `tasks/BACKLOG.md`'s ladder for that lane (respect its GATE lines; stale-check masters older than ~2 days against current main — refresh or flag, never blind-queue). **SALVAGE LIFECYCLE (owner UX ruling 2026-07-06 late): `save/<name>` = re-land pending (counts as waiting, keep visible); when its re-land MERGES, rename to `archive/<name>` in the same fire — archives never count as waiting and never rot anyone's experience. A save/* older than ~3h with a known conflict gets a RE-LAND ruling, not a hand-merge.** **LANE-SAFETY LAW (s61 forensics, 2026-07-06): NEVER refill a lane that has an undrained done-move or a lane branch ahead of main — lane pre-flights `reset --hard`, which DESTROYS unmerged predecessor output (w1-03 light-pass and polish-02 were lost exactly this way; w1-04 survived only as a reflog orphan). Drain the lane first, refill second. Any lane task you AUTHOR must use a drain-gated pre-flight: check the worktree/branch is clean-vs-main before resetting, else STOP and report.** ⚙️ **AND ASK IT WITH THE INSTRUMENT, NOT A FILE-LEVEL EYEBALL (F-1081-9, cured by mechanism s1272 `0db281e0`; the law surface wired up s1278, F-1278-1).** `node scripts/lane-freeze-classify.mjs <branch>` classifies EVERY path the lane’s ahead-commits touched into DUPLICATE / LANE-ONLY / MAIN-ONLY / BOTH-MOVED, **prints the denominator it actually looked at** (`paths=N`), and exits 0 only when the lane holds nothing main has not absorbed (exit 1 = HOLDS n, exit 2 = misuse — read from the code, s1278). ⚠️ **`BOTH-MOVED` is a triage bucket, NOT a loss verdict**: a file lands there whenever main moved at all, *including when main moved by absorbing the lane’s own hunk and adding more.* Ask the one-directional line-level question with `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs <branch> <path> [<path> ...]` — ⓘ **the RUNNABLE form and its argument shape are stated here because the law carried only the bare filename until s2392, while `lane-freeze-classify` has printed the full invocation below its own HOLDS banner all along (F-2392-1, measured: `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs` appeared 0 times across all six LAW_SURFACES). MINOR and deliberately not inflated — the surrounding sentence is imperative, so a reader was always told WHICH tool to ask; what was missing was only how to type it.** s1271 froze three lanes for days on the coarse file-level read and **two of them were never at risk**, while the third held 140 lines main had never seen — a conservative verdict at the wrong resolution is not safety, it is a different way of being wrong. 🚦 **BUT THAT INSTRUMENT ANSWERS THE SAFETY QUESTION, NOT THE REFILL QUESTION — ASK BOTH (F-1298-4, mechanism landed s1299).** `lane-freeze-classify` asks *would a reset LOSE anything?*; a master's pre-flight asks *is this branch clean vs main?* and **STOPs on any unmerged commit, absorbed or not**. s1298 measured `lane/m4` LOSS-FREE, refilled it, and the runner burned **26,940 tokens for zero edits and zero tests** stopping at exactly that line — a lane can be **safe-to-reset and unusable at the same time**, and the fire had no instrument that said so. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs <slot|branch>`** (or `--all` for the fleet). It resolves slot→branch from `git worktree list` itself (**lane slot names are NOT branch names** — ⚠️ **and the mapping ROTS: this parenthetical read "lane-a is `lane/m3`, lane-b `lane/m4`, lane-c `lane/e2-arsenal`, lane-d `lane/perf`" until s1464 measured it FALSE. `git worktree list` shows `worktrees/lane-a..d` checked out on `lane/a`, `lane/b`, `lane/c`, `lane/d`. All eight branches still EXIST, which is what makes this dangerous — the stale names resolve, they just answer about the wrong ref: `lane/m3` reads `ahead=5 behind=347` and `lane/m4` `ahead=7 behind=982`, so a fire measuring by this prose would conclude lanes a and b HOLD unmerged content and refuse to refill two lanes that are actually `ahead=0` USABLE — a false freeze. F-1464-3.** ➡️ **NEVER read the mapping out of this sentence — resolve it from `git worktree list`, which is what `lane-usable.mjs` does itself), and returns one word: **USABLE** (rc 0, `main..branch` empty + clean → refill freely) · **AHEAD-BUT-ABSORBED** (rc 1 — safe to reset but the pre-flight WILL stop; `--cure` archives the tip to `archive/<branch>-s<N>-absorbed-<sha>` **before** resetting, history first, then verifies `main..branch` is empty) · **HOLDS** (rc 2 — content main never absorbed: drain it or rule on the listed paths by hand; `--cure` REFUSES) · **DIRTY** / **BUSY** (rc 2). ⚠️ **Read the WORD, not just the code** — HOLDS and DIRTY and BUSY share rc 2 deliberately, because all three mean *don't refill* while naming three different owed acts. 💀 **AND A BUSY LANE HAS THREE CAUSES, NOT ONE — A CRASHED-RUNNER CORPSE READS EXACTLY LIKE A LIVE RUNNER (F-2366-1, measured and cured s2366).** The BUSY test established WHO holds the slot and **never asked whether that holder is ALIVE**: a dead pid is finite, positive and not one of our ancestors, so it landed on the same verdict a live runner does, and the report printed the same *"a runner holds this slot — leave it alone"*. ⚖️ **`lane-runner-v3.sh` DISAGREES, and it is the authority because it is the pidfile's WRITER: its poll loop tests `kill -0`, and on a dead pid it salvages the run to `tasks/failed/CRASHED-*` and removes the pidfile. Two readers of one file; only the runner's answer was true.** 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — the minted pid was confirmed gone by `kill -0` before anything was believed): a spawned child allowed to EXIT and one still RUNNING both return `true` from `isSlotBusy`, byte-identically.** ⚙️ **REACHABLE exactly when the runner dies mid-run — a reboot (F-2343-1's 37h50m outage), a `kill -9`, a crash — because the self-heal is performed by the thing that died. The window is "as long as the runner is