on. Each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it — stale CLEARS the slot (the Reset Massacre direction) reds arms 1/4/8, EPERM as stale reds arm 5 ALONE, declare-only-on-failure reds arm 9 ALONE, and restoring the original defect (never `stale`) reds 1/3/5/8.** ⚠️ **TWO METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE. (1) The pre-cure file restored VERBATIM reds the SUITE at rc=1 but reports ZERO per-arm reds — it lacks the new exports, so the module will not load. That is a construction refusal, not evidence about staleness, and reporting it as "reds 11 of 11" would have been a false headline.** (2) **My first draft of the live-holder arm used `process.pid` as the "foreign" holder and asserted NOTHING: the CLI is spawned BY the test process, so that pid is genuinely an ancestor and was correctly classified `self`. A live-holder fixture must be a SIBLING of the subject, never one of its ancestors** — the arm went green while measuring the wrong branch entirely, which is this streak's own vacuous-control trap wearing a new costume. 💡 **REUSABLE: when two components read the same file, ask which one WRITES it — that one is the authority, and any divergence is the other one's bug. The runner had the correct test, in shell, all along; the fire's reader simply never adopted it, which is this factory's most-repeated finding (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1) on a new axis: not a cure with no reader, but a cure the SIBLING already had.** ⓘ It reports **tracked dirt and untracked debris separately and never merges them into one number**: `reset --hard` cures the first, `clean -fd` the second, and only tracked dirt can be someone's unsaved work. (The first draft of this very script printed two untracked `.pyc` files under the words "tracked dirt" — caught s1299 by running it on lane-a before trusting it.) 🕳️ **AND THAT INSTRUMENT'S RUN-SURFACE PANEL DECLARED FIFTEEN LANES "byte-identical to main" FROM A CORPUS A SUBDIRECTORY HAD EMPTIED (F-2221-1,