e subdirectory arm reds because playwright run from `scripts/` **fails to find the config and sweeps `scripts/*.test.mjs` instead**. Three accidents and one real mechanism — so the sentence generalised a single subdirectory measurement onto tmpdir, where the cause is entirely different. ⛔ **AND THE STATED EXPOSURE WAS NOT ACTIONABLE AS WRITTEN, which is why it sat unclaimed for 149 fires: `nul-audit.mjs` and `attended-owed-audit.mjs` are NOT test files and cannot be run under `node --test`, so they could never have joined a list this guard feeds to `node --test`. They already carry their own dedicated guards (`nul-audit-corpus-guard`, `attended-owed-anchor-tree-guard`, `lane-usable-pathspec-root-guard`). The real exposure was the HARDCODING.** 🛠️ **CURE, in F-2207-1's inversion — enumerate what to SKIP so the set fails SAFE: the subject set is now DERIVED from an opt-in marker (`@cwd-invariant-collection-guard`) instead of transcribed, each subject ALSO runs from a repo SUBDIRECTORY (the cwd s2220 proved is dangerous, kept alongside tmpdir rather than replacing it), and an EMPTY subject set REFUSES instead of passing vacuously — a `for` loop over nothing registers no assertions and reports success (F-2217-1).** 🚫 **THE MARKER IS AN OPT-IN AND MUST STAY ONE, measured rather than stylistic: a content sniff (`/playwright/ && /--list/`) selects the guard AND its meta-guard, both of which merely quote list-mode, and the guard would then spawn ITSELF — the tautology this repo names in *"a pgrep liveness probe inside the command it probes"*.** ⚙️ **ONE IMPLEMENTATION NOTE WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE, because it is a trap in every meta-guard: the selector had to be EXTRACTED to a plain module (`scripts/collection-guard-subjects.mjs`), because `node:test` REGISTERS TESTS AT MODULE LOAD — so a meta-guard importing the selector from the guard's own test file also ran that file's five playwright arms. Measured: 11 tests / 5.26 s, of which 5 tests and 4.87 s belonged to the file it