t owed"* — every one of them right by the letter and blind by construction. A dry board is the one condition that DOES recur, so the duty now attaches to it as well: **if no drain existed this fire, run the audit.** 💰 **Cost measured s2159 on the live board: 31.9 s** (983 files; the hot path is ~1,966 per-file `git hash-object` / `cat-file -e` subprocess spawns, not the hashing). Against a 35–50 min drain budget that is free **at fire scope**; it is **not** free at **gate scope** (+50% on `test:ledger-guards`, measured 63.7 s green s2159), which is precisely why this is a DUTY here and an **OWNER'S DESK** question there — see **F-2159-1**, filed on the `halo-reextraction-check` / `test:asset-diet` precedent that a ≥30 s battery addition is gate policy, not a fire's drive-by. ⏱️ **AMENDED s2452 (F-2452-1) — THAT FIGURE IS A POINT ESTIMATE WITH NO LOAD CONDITION RECORDED, AND IT IS THE INPUT TO AN OPEN OWNER DECISION.** 📊 **RE-MEASURED s2452 on a genuinely BUSY board — two lane runners mid-gate (`lantern-true-terrain` lane-c, `same-laws-harvest-parity` lane-d, the second 16 min into a playwright battery with chromium at 22–28%), load average **15.40 on 16 CPUs**: **`81.6 s`, rc=0, `AT RISK 0 · LOCAL-ONLY 0`** — **2.6×** the cited 31.9 s. **RESTATED, NOT DELETED: 31.9 s was honest when written and remains the FLOOR.** ⛔ **THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATION IS FORECLOSED BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN WAVED AT: the corpus grew only `983 → 1024` files (+4.2%) across those 293 fires, so per-file cost went `32.5 ms → 79.7 ms` and CORPUS GROWTH ACCOUNTS FOR ~4% OF A 160% MOVE.** The hot path is ~1,966 subprocess spawns, which is exactly the cost that collapses under CPU contention. 🔁 **AND THIS RULE WAS ALREADY ESTABLISHED IN THIS FACTORY, FOR A SIBLING, WITH F-2159-1 NAMED IN ITS OWN TEXT: F-2161-1 measured `test:release-base` at **18.73 s quiet vs 39.4 s at load 12.1 (2.1×)** and wrote *"a drain gate runs exactly when the machine is busiest, so whoever carries **F-2159-1**'s