and then WIDENED the same fire when it proved too narrow, which is the more useful half of the story.** First pass asked *which guards read the live LEDGER* and found six: `goal-tracker.test.mjs` (live `goals.json` **and** `tasks/done` — the second source was not in the finding) · `goal-closure-reason.test.mjs` · `ruling-propagation-guard.test.mjs` **and** `ruling-propagation-guard.mjs` · `findings-state-guard.mjs` · `blocker-panel-closed-guard.mjs` · `citation-title-guard.mjs`. ⚠️ **Then s1301's own bookkeeping reddened `test:node-guards` in two ways that those six are blind to** — `gate-caller-audit` (the new npm script was an un-rooted gate) and `law-pointer-guard` (inserting a comment block pushed `workers: isFireShell` from `:30` to `:50`, rotting the coordinate that **this very clause** and `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` both cite). Neither reads the ledger; both were caught only by the full battery. ➡️ **So the real class is not "guards that read the ledger" — it is GUARDS WHOSE SUBJECT A FIRE MUTATES LATE IN ITS OWN RUN**, and a fire mutates three such subjects: ledger rows, **law surfaces** (`scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**` — every coordinate in them rots when anyone inserts a line above it), and **gate topology** (a new npm script must be rooted or grandfathered with a reason). `test:ledger-guards` is now those **eight** as of s1301, since GROWN DELIBERATELY — **RE-MEASURED s1539 BY RUNNING IT: 13 node test files carrying 97 tests (97 pass / 0 fail, 15.3 s) PLUS 11 chained leaves — ⚠️ ALL FOUR FIGURES SUPERSEDED s2099, SEE THE CORRECTION BELOW** (findings-state, blocker-panel, ruling-propagation, citations, desk-declaration, status-archive-audit, attended-owed-audit, main-lock-gate-guard.sh, janitor-request-rejection.sh, lane-dispatch-safety-guard.sh, nul-audit). The figures this clause carried until now — *eleven guards / 12 leaf files / 38 tests*, true when written (s1334 desk-declaration, s1341 status-archive-audit, s1343 attended-owed-audit) — are RESTATED not deleted, because the drift is the provenance: the set has grown ~2.5x in tests and nothing decayed. 📈 **RE-MEASURED AGAIN s2099 (F-2099-1) — EVERY ONE OF THOSE FOUR FIGURES HAS DRIFTED TOO, IN THE SAME DIRECTION: `18` node test files carrying `180` tests (`180 pass / 0 fail / 0 skipped`, **`49.9 s`**) PLUS **`17`** chained leaves** (was 13 / 97 / 15.3 s / 11 at s1539 — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, per the Retention Law: the drift is the provenance, and it is the second cost citation this one fire found stale in the same clause-family). 📈 **RE-MEASURED AGAIN s2414 (F-2414-1) — ALL FOUR FIGURES HAVE DRIFTED A THIRD TIME, AND THIS TIME IT IS THIS CLAUSE’S OWN CLOSING INSTRUCTION THAT WENT UNEXECUTED: `97` node test files carrying `948` assertions (`948 pass / 0 fail`, **`77.2 s`** measured s2414) PLUS **`20`** chained leaves, the last of which contributes a further `83` bash legs** (was `18` / `180` / `49.9 s` / `17` at s2099 — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, because the sequence IS the finding: files `13 → 18 → 97`, assertions `97 → 180 → 948`, leaves `11 → 17 → 20`). ⚖️ **PRICED HONESTLY, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS READING IS WRONG AND s2413 GOT THIS RIGHT: it did not get SLOWER, it got BIGGER — assertions grew `5.3×` while wall grew `~1.7×`, so cost PER ASSERTION FELL ~68%. That is a healthy battery growing exactly as F-1300-4 intends, and the growth must NEVER be pruned back to make a number here true.** ⏱️ **BUT CITE A FLOOR AND A RANGE, NEVER A MEAN — THIS CORRECTS s2413 AS WELL AS s2099: that fire measured four BACK-TO-BACK runs inside one fire (`87.7 · 97.7 · 96.5 · 99.3`, mean `95.3 s`) and I measured `77.2 s` in the next fire on an idle board, so the observed spread across two fires is `77–99 s`, ~29% wide. A mean taken inside ONE fire measures that fire’s load, not the battery. Treat `~75 s` as the FLOOR, budget `~100 s`, and expect both to grow. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2478 UNDER A NAMED LOAD CONDITION, WHICH IS THE ONLY KIND OF MEASUREMENT THIS FIG