de: 0` as evidence the class is obsolete.** It is a transient state by construction: any fire recording a readiness hold mints a new one, and the surviving 5 owner-fork + 1 disputed are exactly the residue that no green battery can lift. ⚠️ **Verify the fate before celebrating a shrinking census** — "gone from the blocked list" and "merged" are different facts, and only the second is good news; s1538 checked the mergeHash rather than the absence. The discriminator used to live ONLY in free prose, so it could be — and was — silently omitted: s1381's handoff records flipping "all three" of its leaves with an explicit *"GATE-SIDE HOLD, NOT AN OWNER GATE"* reason *"so the next fire cannot misread them as owner forks"*, and **the label reached TWO of the three**. The leaf it missed, `e1-authored-bundle-validation`, is the one **s1381 and s1382 each named as the #1 priority and "the cheapest real merge on the board"** — and this tool then asserted the owner-only reading on its behalf, freezing it. ⚠️ **The field changes NO exit code** — every class still exits 1 and still refuses the drain; it changes only who is told they can lift it. An undeclared or unknown class **fails safe to `owner-fork`**, so forgetting it can never unblock anything, and `scripts/block-class-guard.test.mjs` (in `test:ledger-guards`) reds if any blocked leaf omits it. 📐 **HOW THE "do not drain, do not gate" STOP ABOVE APPLIES PER CLASS, stated because s1383 introduced the distinction into a clause that predates it and must not leave it ambiguous.** For **`owner-fork`** the STOP is total and unchanged — do not gate, because gating an owner fork only builds a case toward merging the thing the owner reserved, which is precisely the rf-34 failure. For **`gate-side`** the STOP is **on the MERGE**: gathering evidence in a **detached scratch worktree** (§3.0b) is reversible, touches nothing, and IS the prescribed cure — but the merge becomes lawful only when the same fire **also flips the leaf out of `blo