 by their live leaf** — this one (`queued`), and `…s1176-blocked-by-26-cell-master-divergence-…-eight-winds-wiring-hero.md`, whose leaf reads `merged` at `29bac3d9478e9f3a77385c75965b60447a90500c`. ⚠️ **The cost is not a stale paragraph — it is an armed Silent No-Op (Mistake #1):** the stopped run that filename names **left zero diff** by its own preserved record, so a successor obeying the instruction gates a run that changed nothing, and looks for a `blocked` leaf to flip that does not exist. 🚫 **No mechanism is proposed and the reason is the point: the obvious one — have this tool print the filename-vs-leaf disagreement — is cheap and purely mechanical and STILL would not have prevented this, because s1665 never ran the tool.** It had the filename in hand and read its words. **A tool only helps the fire that asks it** (the F-1582-1 gap: the misread happens while a fire reads with its eyes, a moment no battery observes). ➡️ **The cure is therefore the one command you already have, invoked at the moment of ASSERTION rather than the moment of action — and it generalises: any name the factory mints to describe a state (done-move prefixes, `save/` vs `archive/`, row markers) is a SNAPSHOT of a claim, while the ledger IS the claim. On disagreement the ledger wins and the name is what must be re-read.**\012**§3.0b CUSTODY — NEVER PLACE CONTENT YOU HAVE NOT *DECIDED* TO MERGE INTO MAIN'S WORKING TREE. GATE UNDECIDED CONTENT IN A DETACHED WORKTREE (F-1295-1, s1295 — MADE STANDING s1342).** ⚠️ **This cure was declared "adopted" by exactly one fire and then written into NO law surface for 47 fires** — re-measured s1342 across `scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**`, `AGENTS.md` and every `^- **s9` bullet in `STATUS.md` (**140 total = 18 genuine lettered law bullets** — `s9i`/`s9ab`/`s9al`/`s9ar`/… — **plus 119 numeric handoff archives and 3 bare `- **s9 ` VM-era env bullets (obsolete on native, per the blanket VM-era clause)**; the loose phrase "140 law bullets" would over-state the law corpus 7.8×, so it is written out here — ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538, 196 fires later: EXACTLY 140 / 18 / 119 / 3, every figure unchanged. DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS ONE AGAIN.** It is stable *by construction*, which is the part worth knowing: the `^- **s9` pattern matches only the closed historical range s900–s999, so it cannot grow no matter how many fires run — today's archives are minted as `- **s15NN`. A figure that looks like a growing census is in fact a frozen one, and this line now says so): the only "detached worktree" in the corpus is `CLAUDE.md:50` (Mistake #12), which is about **contention**, a different question F-1295-1 itself explicitly distinguishes. §3's clean-main rule asks whether *someone else's* dirt contaminates your gate; **this asks the inverse — whether YOUR gate leaks.** 🎯 **THE INCIDENT:** s1294 checked four lane paths into main's tree to evaluate a slice, wrote **VERDICT: HOLD — NOT MERGED**, reverted with `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>`, confirmed `git status` clean, and was **wrong** — a concurrent attended session's broad `git add` had already committed all four at **`b37c1fc6`**, under a commit message about a rehearsal that named none of them, **thirteen minutes before the handoff denying it.** 🔑 **WHY THE VERIFICATION COULD NOT WORK, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE HALF: every probe that fire had returns the SAME answer whether the revert worked or the content was committed out from under it.** `git status` cannot detect a sweep, because **a sweep's signature is the ABSENCE of dirt**; and `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` restores *from* a HEAD that has itself moved, so the "revert" **re-installed** the very content it was undoing. The only discriminating probe is a **blob-hash comparison against a PRE-sweep ref** — which is why F-1295-1 is recorded in hashes. ⚖️ **A HOLD verdict has NO ENFORCEMENT SURFACE:** it lives in a review file and a goal leaf, and **nothing in git defends it**. The same window ships an **owner-BLOCKED** slice just as readily — the version that costs a design fork, and the one §3.0 exists to prevent. **What must NOT be concluded is "the sweep turned out fine, so the sweep is fine":** that slice was exonerated by a control run *afterwards*, and had it gone the other way the defect would have shipped past a verdict that said no, with the fire that wrote the verdict still believing it held. Cost of the cure: one `git worktree add` plus a `node_modules` symlink. ⓘ **SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT ME: F-1295-1 measured the WORKING-TREE case only.** Gating by `git merge --no-ff` and planning to revert on a red (§3, "revert the merge") puts undecided content on **HEAD** rather than in the working tree — structurally the same bet against concurrency, but **UNMEASURED as of s1342**; treat it as a caution, not a finding, and do not cite it as proven. ✅ **NO LONGER UNMEASURED — THE HEAD-SIDE CASE WAS MEASURED s1589 AND THE CAUTION IS NOW A FINDING (F-1589-5).** s1589 left drain 2's merge staged in main's index while writing its review; **ninety seconds later** a concurrent Cowork agent committed **`9371c1c8d`** — parents `e15858b13` (the fire's own stamp commit) and `153d30ba6` (the lane tip) — sweeping the entire staged merge into a commit headlined *"the operator falls at twin-banks w16 (697 kills) — notebook gen 2"*, which names none of it. ⓘ **Nothing was lost and nothing was corrupt** (`main..lane/b` empty, artifacts byte-identical, the fire's other drain untouched) — **the casualty was attribution, and the EXPOSURE is far worse than the outcome: had that gate gone RED, the plan was `merge --abort`, and the sweep would have SHIPPED the rejected content anyway.** That is the same failure as F-1295-1 with the staging area swapped for the index, and it retires the "structurally the same bet" hedge above: it is not merely structurally the same, it has now happened. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: never leave a merge staged on main.** Gate undecided content in the detached worktree exactly as this section already mandates, then **merge and commit as ONE act** — `git merge --no-ff <branch> -m "…"`, never `--no-commit` followed by review-writing. The review file, ledger rows and goal leaf land in a FOLLOWING commit; that is already the house sequence (§3.0's two-commit rule exists because a commit cannot contain its own hash), so this costs nothing and closes the window. ⚠️ **Do not read "the sweep turned out fine" as evidence the window is safe** — that is the exact reasoning this section already forbids one paragraph above, and s1589 is the second fire to be saved by luck rather than by design.\012**§3.1 EVERY FIRE-SIDE PLAYWRIGHT COMMAND PASSES `--workers=1`. NOT AN OPTIMISATION — A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT OF THE INSTRUMENT (F-1270-1, measured s1270).** Until `d1a0846d` `playwright.config.ts` set no `workers` key, so a fire's default was **6 obtained workers**, and at 6 the fire shell's per-job CPU ceiling (F-1269-1: the ceiling is the fire's process context, measured 7.53× lane vs 3.47× fire at eight children) starves each chromium until timing-sensitive assertions go red. **Interleaved, same shell, same hour, s1270: `--workers=1` → 3/3 runs rc=0, 18/18 instances passed, 0 drift reds · default → 3/3 runs rc=1, 17 drift reds / 18.** ⭐ **And it costs nothing, which kills the only objection: w=1 mean wall 55.94 s vs default 60.74 s — the serial arm is 7.9% FASTER in the fire shell**, because the parallel arm is fighting the ceiling. ⚠️ **This is a factory-gate correctness issue, not a game bug: every fire-side gate ever run at default workers was a known-unreliable instrument, and its reds were the shell's, not the slice's.** `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md:33` has carried the flag since s114 — incidentally, with no stated reason, which is exactly why six fires never knew it was load-bearing; this clause states the reason so it cannot decay again. ✅ **THE LAW NOW HAS A MECHANISM, SO THE FLAG IS A BELT-AND-BRACES, NOT THE ONLY DEFENCE (F-1270-3, landed s1270 `d1a0846d`): `playwright.config.ts:50` carries `workers: isFireShell ? 1 : undefined`, keyed on `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` being PRESENT** (**was `:30`; re-based s1301 when the F-1296-3 `testIgnore` comment block was inserted above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts) — launchd sets it to `~/.claude-fires` and `scripts/fire-runner.sh:130` (**was `:129` — re-based s2500 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`129 → 130`, **+1**), the rot caused by the ATTENDED commit `304ff2396` (2026-09-04T12:45:12, the codex engine switch) landing MID-FIRE and inserting the WALL-on-primary echo directly above it. The cited line is INTACT and still supports the claim — re-read s2500: `:130` is the `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-alt"` alt-fire invocation, so `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` is still SET for fires and the `workers: isFireShell` key still holds. ⚠️ **Two sibling pointers rotted by the same +1 in the same commit (`CLAUDE.md` → `:136→:137`, `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` → `:129→:130`) — re-grep EACH, never carry one delta across three surfaces; they agreed here only by luck of a single-line insertion.** `:126`, `:121` same-day (+5,+3), `:102`, `:86`, `:81` before that; rotted attended 2026-08-12 by exactly 19 lines when the owner-ruled fire-engine switch landed above it, ALT line re-verified intact; before that rotted s1536 by exactly 16 lines when the F-1536-1 dry-board-guard cure replaced the hardcoded lane list at `:63`, and before that s1456 by 5 lines when the owner's `02bea28c` auth fix inserted the `.env.local` token block above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**) to `~/.claude-alt`, while lanes and attended sessions never set it, so **lanes keep full parallelism** (the lane shell runs 6 workers ~3.5× FASTER — F-1267-1 — and must not pay for a fire-only defect). Still pass `--workers=1` explicitly: it is free, it survives anyone editing the config, and it keeps the intent legible at the call site. 🚫 **What remains forbidden is